### For "make the stock and content names match the rest": should `content/` objects get extensions too, or only `stock/`?

You checked "Make the stock and content names match the rest". In the media bucket, every picture is named `<sha256>.<ext>`, with `<sha256>-thumb.jpg` beside it. Two folders don't follow that:
- **`content/`** holds every document's bytes (34,757 objects, 10.7 GiB). Each object is `content/<sha256>` with no extension, and its type is in the `mime` column of the `CONTENT` row that names it.
- **`stock/`** is image search's cache. It names objects by an ID (a hash of the source and the source's picture ID), not by their bytes, and uses no extension except on the records.

The item's text called `content/`'s missing extension deliberate and only proposed changing `stock/`. So I'm checking before I rename 10.7 GiB.

Real example: `CONTENT` row `cntcIMMRPajGGz`, `mime` = `application/pdf`, size 259,292.

- (*) Rename stock only; leave content as it is
  `CONTENT.blob` stays `content/cf11d90fb0f2…ea2`, and the row's `mime` keeps saying it's a PDF. No migration.
- ( ) Give content objects extensions too
  `CONTENT.blob` becomes `content/cf11d90fb0f2…ea2.pdf`. A one-off tool in `local/` would copy all 34,757 objects to the new names and set `blob` on 34,739 `CONTENT` rows; you'd run it after the deploy. It needs a fixed extension for every `mime` in use (`message/rfc822` → `.eml`, `text/markdown` → `.md`, `text/html` → `.html`, …). Every writer of `content/` (mail intake, invitations, fetcher, extractor, wiki, calendar import) would then build the name from the type.

ian@fc.run chose: Rename stock only; leave content as it is

### To give stock pictures extensions, should import stop keeping its own copy of the picture under `stock/`?

You checked "Make the stock and content names match the rest". Image search (the "Find an image" picker) keeps three objects per stock hit, all named by an ID that hashes the source and its picture ID. Real example, ID `343713c6…101f`:
- `stock/343713c6…101f.json`: the hit's record (source URLs, credit)
- `stock/343713c6…101f-thumb`: the source's thumbnail, any image type, no extension
- `stock/343713c6…101f`: the full picture, 189,655 bytes, no extension, kept when someone imports it

Importing then stores the same bytes again in the app's folder under their own hash: `wiki-images/1645a6d7…b0.jpg` (189,655 bytes).

Both choices make the thumbnail `stock/<id>-thumb.jpg`, re-encoded to JPEG with the same `blob.Thumbnail` every other thumbnail uses, so its name never depends on what the source sent. The question is the full picture. Its name has to come from the ID, because import looks it up by ID before it has any bytes.

- (*) Drop stock's copy of the picture
  Import downloads from the source each time and keeps only the app-folder copy (`wiki-images/1645a6d7…b0.jpg`), which it already writes. `stock/` then holds only `<id>.json` and `<id>-thumb.jpg`. A second import of the same hit downloads again; imports are limited to 20 an hour per person. Unsplash's download notice then goes out on every import, which is what its guidelines ask for.
- ( ) Keep the copy as `stock/<id>.<ext>`
  The picture becomes `stock/343713c6…101f.jpg`. Import finds it by listing `stock/343713c6…101f.`, which also matches the `.json` record, and picks the one that isn't JSON: one bucket list call per import, and a little more code.

Either way, the existing 2,523 old `-thumb` objects and 23 extension-less pictures would be deleted by hand after the deploy (a `gcloud storage rm` I'd type into the terminal for you).

